{
  "title": "Intraoperative detection of oral squamous cell carcinoma margins in dogs using cathepsin-activated near-infrared (NIR) imaging – University of Pennsylvania, School of Veterinary Medicine",
  "text": "Intraoperative detection of oral squamous cell carcinoma margins in dogs using cathepsin-activated near-infrared (NIR) imaging – University of Pennsylvania, School of Veterinary Medicine\nSkip to main content\nSkip to veterinary school Navigation\nSkip to veterinary hospital Navigation\nGo to site search\nUniversity of Pennsylvania, School of Veterinary Medicine logo\n- - -\nSchool of Veterinary Medicine\nBack\nPrograms\nBack\nVMD Curriculum\nLearn more\nPrograms\nVMD Degree\nThe Veterinariae Medicinae Doctoris (VMD) degree is designed to prepare students for careers in veterinary practice, research, and public health.\nDual Degree Programs\nVMD | PhD\nVMD | MSW\nVMD | MPH\nVMD | ML\nVMD | MBA\nVMD | MES\nVMD | MSTR\nVMD Admissions\nHow to Apply\nTimeline\nTuition and Aid\nContact Admissions\nOne Health\nOther Programs\nOnline Graduate Programs in Animal Welfare & Behavior\nCertificates & Continuing Education\nStudent Life\nBack\nStudent Life\nLearn More\nLife Outside of Class\nLearn More\nStudent Life\nLife Outside of Class\nTraining Opportunities\nStudent Stories\nCareer Counseling\nAlumni\nDepartments & Faculty\nBack\nAcademic Departments\nLearn More\nAcademic Departments\nBiomedical Sciences\nClinical Sciences & Advanced Medicine\nClinical Studies – New Bolton Center\nPathobiology\nFaculty Directory\nResearch\nBack\nResearch at Penn Vet\nLearn More\nResearch\nCenters\nCenter for Animal Transgenesis & Germ Cell Research\nCenter for Stewardship Agriculture and Food Security\nSwine Teaching and Research Center\nPenn Vet Cancer Center\nPenn Vet Working Dog Center\nInstitute\nInstitute for Infectious & Zoonotic Diseases\nResearch Programs\nComparative Immunotherapy Program\nDivision of  Experimental Retinal Therapies\nShelter Medicine and Community Engagement Program\nThe Swine Group\nWildlife Futures Program\nResearch Laboratories\nCore Labs\nAbout\nBack\nNews\nLearn More\nOffice of the Dean\nLearn More\nAbout\nNews\nOffice of the Dean\nEvents\nOne Health\nGiving\nCommunity and Engagement\nBellwether Magazine\nAccreditation\nContact\nVeterinary Hospitals\nBack\nVeterinary Hospitals\nLearn More\nRyan Hospital\nCare for cats, dogs & other companion animals\nBy Appointment\n215-746-8387\nFor Emergencies\n215-746-8911\nFind Us\n3900 Spruce Street Philadelphia, PA 19104\nNew Bolton Center Hospital\nCare for horses & livestock/farm animals\nFor Appointments & Emergencies\n610-444-5800\nFind Us\n382 West Street Road Kennett Square, PA 19348\nApply\nAlumni\nGive\nContact Penn Vet\nApply\nAlumni\nGive\nContact Penn Vet\nSearch.....\nHomepage\nClinical Trials\nIntraoperative detection of oral squamous cell carcinoma margins in dogs using cathepsin-activated near-infrared (NIR) imaging\nOctober 21, 2026\nSquamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is a common cancer of the epidermis, or outer layer of the skin, and oral tissues in dogs. The skin, oral cavity, and digits are the most common sites of SCC. In this study, we will be assessing dogs with oral SCC. Surgical excision of the tumor is regarded as the best treatment option for local disease control.\nThe aim of this study is to improve detection of surgical margins at the time of surgery to remove the tumor. This is vital in both veterinary and human surgical oncology to minimize the chance of local recurrence. We are using a new near-infrared (NIR) imaging agent, injected intravenously, and a novel imaging system to identify the tumor and its margins during surgery. This imaging adds no more than 15 minutes to a surgical procedure.\nEligibility:\nDogs with diagnosed oral SCC that are eligible for surgical excision\nNo prior/current treatments (including surgery, radiation, and chemotherapy) for this cancer\nNo other significant health concerns that are an immediate threat to life outside of trial.\nAbility to attend visits at PennVet in Philadelphia\nBenefits:\n$3000 paid towards the cost of surgery.\nContact the VCIC at 215-573-0302 or email\nvcic@vet.upenn.edu\nfor more information.\nUniversity of Pennsylvania, School of Veterinary Medicine logo\nApply\nContact Penn Vet\nGive\nFind us\nSchool of Veterinary Medicine\n3800 Spruce Street,\nPhiladelphia, PA 19104\nSee Our Veterinary Hospitals\nFollow Us\nSECTIONS\nAbout\nAccreditation\nCareers at Penn Vet\nEmergency Info\nFaculty Directory\nInside.PennVet Portal\nMedia and Press Inquiries\nPenn Vet Library\nPenn Vet News\nPrograms\nResearch\nStudent Life\nReport Accessibility Issues\nUniversity of Pennsylvania\nPrivacy Policy\n© 2026 University of Pennsylvania, School of Veterinary Medicine",
